# Line endings.
#
# THE PLUGINS ARE INSTALLED AS A GIT CLONE, ON THE USER'S MACHINE, WITH THE
# USER'S GIT CONFIG. Git for Windows installs with core.autocrlf=true by
# default, so without these rules every shell script in this repo reaches a
# Windows user with CRLF endings — and bash reads the trailing \r as part of the
# command: `set -u` fails as `set: -: invalid option`, and values carry a stray
# \r that breaks comparisons without any error at all. That covers every skill
# script in every kit, and pm-kit's SessionStart hook.
#
# `text eol=lf` pins LF in the working tree on every platform, whatever
# core.autocrlf says, and binaries are still detected and left alone.
#
# EVERYTHING, not only shell scripts. The first version of this file pinned just
# the scripts, and the Windows CI job found the rest the same day: a shebang
# `.mjs` checked out CRLF fails to load under vitest ("Invalid or unexpected
# token" — its shebang stripping does not expect \r), and every SKILL.md
# frontmatter reader written as /^---\n/ finds nothing. This repo is authored
# on Linux and read by tools that assume \n; Windows editors handle LF fine.

* text=auto eol=lf

# The one kind of file that genuinely needs CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Anything bash runs.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf
*.sh.disabled   text eol=lf
**/.githooks/** text eol=lf

# make treats a trailing \r in a recipe as part of the command.
Makefile        text eol=lf
*.mk            text eol=lf
